# TuningFork in a notebook

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tuning_fork, "~> 0.1"},
  {:tuning_fork_samples, "~> 0.1"},
  {:tuning_fork_kino, "~> 0.1"}
])
```

## One tune, three ways

A tune in D minor at 100 bpm: kick, snare and hats from a Roland TR-909, a fingered bass,
electric piano stabs on Dm, B♭, F and C, a flute line over the top and strings underneath.
The three cells below are it written three ways, so the spellings can be compared with the
sound held constant. The grid holds its two-bar core; the two live spellings take that core
through an arrangement — strings and chords alone, then drums and bass, the flute over them,
a breakdown with an echoing line, then everything with an arpeggio on top — about seventy
seconds before it comes round again. Press **▶ listen** on a cell's stage once, then play.

## Compose

A step sequencer you click. Each row is a track — a drum, an instrument or a recording — and
each cell a step: a number is a scale degree, a longer cell a held note. **Play** loops the
grid, and the cell writes the same music out as `TuningFork` source underneath, so a grid
becomes code you can keep editing by hand. The grid is eight beats of four steps.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoTuningFork.ComposerCell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
import TuningFork.Part

alias TuningFork.{Gm, Score, Voice}

base =
  Voice.new(
    shape: :saw,
    gain: 0.5,
    cutoff: 0.45,
    envelope: TuningFork.Envelope.new(attack: 0.006, sustain: 0.3, release: 0.12)
  )

kit = Gm.drums()
bass = Gm.for_program(33, base)
piano = Gm.for_program(0, base)
pipe = Gm.for_program(73, base)
strings = Gm.for_program(48, base)

drum_kick =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: kit[36], gain: 0.9)
  |> repeat(2, fn bar -> steps(bar, "x.......x.x.....x.......x.x..x..", 1 / 4) end)

drum_snare =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: kit[38], gain: 0.6)
  |> repeat(2, fn bar -> steps(bar, "....x.......x.......x.......x...", 1 / 4) end)

drum_clap =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: kit[39], gain: 0.4)
  |> repeat(2, fn bar -> steps(bar, "............x...............x...", 1 / 4) end)

drum_hat =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: kit[42], gain: 0.35)
  |> repeat(2, fn bar -> steps(bar, "x.x.x.x.x.x.x...x.x.x.x.x.x.x...", 1 / 4) end)

drum_open_hat =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: kit[46], gain: 0.3)
  |> repeat(2, fn bar -> steps(bar, "..............x...............x.", 1 / 4) end)

pitched_bass =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: bass, gain: 0.6)
  |> repeat(2, fn bar ->
    steps(
      bar,
      [
        {:d2, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        :d2,
        {:d2, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        :a2,
        nil,
        {:as2, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        :as2,
        {:as2, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        :f2,
        nil,
        {:f2, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        :f2,
        {:f2, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        :c3,
        nil,
        {:c3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        :c3,
        {:c3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        :g2,
        nil
      ],
      1 / 4,
      release: 1.0
    )
  end)

pitched_piano =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: piano, gain: 0.45)
  |> repeat(2, fn bar ->
    steps(
      bar,
      [
        {:d3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:d3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:d3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:d3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:c3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:c3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:c3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:c3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil
      ],
      1 / 4,
      release: 1.0
    )
  end)

pitched_piano_2 =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: piano, gain: 0.45)
  |> repeat(2, fn bar ->
    steps(
      bar,
      [
        {:f3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:f3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:f3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:f3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:f3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:f3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:e3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:e3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil
      ],
      1 / 4,
      release: 1.0
    )
  end)

pitched_piano_3 =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: piano, gain: 0.45)
  |> repeat(2, fn bar ->
    steps(
      bar,
      [
        {:a3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:a3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:as3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:as3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:a3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:a3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:g3, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:g3, release: 2 / 4},
        nil
      ],
      1 / 4,
      release: 1.0
    )
  end)

pitched_pipe =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: pipe, gain: 0.5)
  |> repeat(2, fn bar ->
    steps(
      bar,
      [
        {:a4, release: 3 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        :f4,
        {:d4, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:f4, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:g4, release: 3 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        :f4,
        {:d4, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:f4, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:a4, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        {:c5, release: 3 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        :a4,
        {:g4, release: 4 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:e4, release: 2 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil
      ],
      1 / 4,
      release: 0.8
    )
  end)

pitched_strings =
  part(bpm: 100, synth: strings, gain: 0.25)
  |> repeat(2, fn bar ->
    steps(
      bar,
      [
        {:d3, release: 8 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:as3, release: 8 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:f3, release: 8 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        {:c4, release: 8 / 4},
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil,
        nil
      ],
      1 / 4,
      release: 2.0
    )
  end)

song =
  Score.from_parts(
    [
      drum_kick,
      drum_snare,
      drum_clap,
      drum_hat,
      drum_open_hat,
      pitched_bass,
      pitched_piano,
      pitched_piano_2,
      pitched_piano_3,
      pitched_pipe,
      pitched_strings
    ],
    bpm: 100,
    beats: 16
  )

song
|> Score.render(44100)
|> TuningFork.Fx.reverb(44100, room: 0.25, mix: 0.2)
|> TuningFork.Wav.encode(rate: 44100)
|> Kino.Audio.new(:wav)
```

## Live patterns, the Strudel way

One pattern a row, stacked and looped. Here a cycle is two beats — one chord — so `<a b c d>`
walks the four chords and `cps` 0.8333 is 100 bpm. The arrangement is in the masks: each
digit of `mask("<0 1 1 1 0 1 1>/8")` switches its row on or off for eight cycles, so the
seven digits are the seven sections. Edit a row and press **Evaluate** (or ctrl+enter): the
change lands at the next cycle line. A row behind `--` is switched off; a row ending in
`|> scope()` or `|> pianoroll()` draws itself. `bank("RolandTR909")` plays the drum machine's
recordings once they have arrived, the kit's own drums until then. A piece pasted from
strudel.cc plays as written — see `strudel.livemd`.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoTuningFork.LivePatternsCell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
unless Process.whereis(TuningFork.Stage), do: Kino.render(KinoTuningFork.stage())

rows = [
  %{
    source:
      "s(\"<[bd ~] [[bd ~ bd ~] ~] [bd ~] [[bd ~ bd ~] [~ bd ~ ~]]>, ~ sd, <~ [~ cp] ~ [~ cp]>\")"
  },
  %{source: "|> bank(\"RolandTR909\") |> mask(\"<0 1 1 1 0 1 1>/8\") |> gain(0.35)"},
  %{
    source:
      "s(\"<hh*4 [hh hh hh oh]>\") |> bank(\"RolandTR909\") |> mask(\"<[0 1] 1 1 1 1 1 1>/8\") |> gain(0.25)"
  },
  %{
    source:
      "n(\"<[0 0 4] [5 5 2] [2 2 6] [6 6 3]>\") |> scale(\"d2:minor\") |> struct(\"x@2 ~ x x@2 x ~\")"
  },
  %{source: "|> s(\"gm_electric_bass_finger\") |> mask(\"<0 1 1 1 1 1 1>/8\") |> gain(0.5)"},
  %{
    source: "note(\"<[d3,f3,a3] [d3,f3,bb3] [c3,f3,a3] [c3,e3,g3]>\") |> struct(\"x@4 ~@2 x@2\")"
  },
  %{source: "|> s(\"gm_epiano1\") |> phaser(2) |> room(0.4) |> gain(0.3)"},
  %{source: "note(\"<[a4@3 f4 d4@2 f4@2] [g4@3 f4 d4@4] [f4@2 a4@2 c5@3 a4] [g4@4 e4@2 ~@2]>\")"},
  %{source: "|> s(\"gm_flute\") |> mask(\"<0 0 1 1 0 1 1>/8\") |> room(0.3) |> gain(0.4)"},
  %{
    source:
      "note(\"<[d5@2 ~ a4 ~@3 f4@2] [~@3 e4 f4@2 d4@2]>\") |> s(\"gm_flute\") |> delay(0.3) |> room(0.6)"
  },
  %{source: "|> mask(\"<0 0 0 0 1 0 0>/8\") |> gain(0.4)"},
  %{
    source:
      "note(\"<[d4 f4 a4 d5] [d4 f4 bb4 d5] [c4 f4 a4 c5] [c4 e4 g4 c5]>*2\") |> s(\"gm_epiano1\")"
  },
  %{source: "|> mask(\"<0 0 0 0 0 1 1>/8\") |> pan(\"<0.3 -0.3>\") |> gain(0.2)"},
  %{source: "note(\"<d3 bb3 f3 c4>\") |> s(\"gm_string_ensemble_1\") |> room(0.5) |> gain(0.3)"},
  %{source: "-- |> pianoroll()"}
]

pattern = TuningFork.Session.combined(rows)

if TuningFork.Stage.cycle() do
  TuningFork.Stage.update_pattern(pattern, at: :cycle)
else
  TuningFork.Stage.start_pattern(pattern, cps: 0.8333)
end
```

## Live loops, the Sonic Pi way

Named loops, each on its own length, in Sonic Pi's words — `sample`, `play`, `sleep`,
`play_pattern_timed`, `with_fx` — and ordinary Elixir around them. Every loop but the pad is
two bars long and reads its section from `tick()`, its own round counter, against the same
`"ABCCDEE"` the masks spell above. Evaluate a loop and it swaps in when it next comes round.
`use_synth "gm_epiano1"` plays a General MIDI instrument through the kit; `use_synth :tb303`
and the other synths are there too.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoTuningFork.LiveLoopsCell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
unless Process.whereis(TuningFork.Stage), do: Kino.render(KinoTuningFork.stage())

use TuningFork.SonicPi

live_loop :drums do
  use_bpm 100
  section = String.at("ABCCDEE", div(rem(tick(), 14), 2))
  use_sample_defaults amp: if(section in ~w(B C E), do: 0.6, else: 0.0)

  for _bar <- 1..2 do
    sample :bd_haus
    sleep 1
    sample :sn_dolf
    sleep 1
    sample :bd_haus
    sleep 0.5
    sample :bd_haus
    sleep 0.5
    sample :sn_dolf
    sample :perc_snap
    sleep 1
  end
end

live_loop :hats do
  use_bpm 100
  section = String.at("ABCCDEE", div(rem(tick(), 14), 2))
  level = if section == "A", do: 0.2, else: 0.4

  for step <- 0..15 do
    sample if(step == 15, do: :drum_cymbal_open, else: :drum_cymbal_closed), amp: level
    sleep 0.5
  end
end

live_loop :bass do
  use_bpm 100
  use_synth "gm_electric_bass_finger"
  section = String.at("ABCCDEE", div(rem(tick(), 14), 2))

  if section == "A" do
    sleep 8
  else
    play_pattern_timed [
                         :d2,
                         :d2,
                         :d2,
                         :a2,
                         :bb2,
                         :bb2,
                         :bb2,
                         :f2,
                         :f2,
                         :f2,
                         :f2,
                         :c3,
                         :c3,
                         :c3,
                         :c3,
                         :g2
                       ],
                       [0.75, 0.25, 0.5, 0.5], release: 0.3
  end
end

live_loop :chords do
  use_bpm 100
  use_synth "gm_epiano1"
  use_synth_defaults amp: 0.6

  for notes <- [[:d3, :f3, :a3], [:d3, :f3, :bb3], [:c3, :f3, :a3], [:c3, :e3, :g3]] do
    play_chord notes, release: 1
    sleep 1.5
    play_chord notes, release: 0.5
    sleep 0.5
  end
end

live_loop :melody do
  use_bpm 100
  use_synth "gm_flute"
  tune = [:a4, :f4, :d4, :f4, :g4, :f4, :d4, :f4, :a4, :c5, :a4, :g4, :e4]
  times = [0.75, 0.25, 0.5, 0.5, 0.75, 0.25, 1, 0.5, 0.5, 0.75, 0.25, 1, 1]

  case String.at("ABCCDEE", div(rem(tick(), 14), 2)) do
    "C" ->
      play_pattern_timed tune, times, release: 0.4

    "E" ->
      play_pattern_timed tune, times, release: 0.4, amp: 1.2

    "D" ->
      with_fx :echo, phase: 0.375, mix: 0.3 do
        play_pattern_timed [:d5, :a4, :f4, :e4, :f4, :d4], [1, 0.5, 2.5, 0.5, 1, 2.5],
          release: 0.5
      end

    _ ->
      sleep 8
  end
end

live_loop :arpeggio do
  use_bpm 100
  use_synth "gm_epiano1"

  if String.at("ABCCDEE", div(rem(tick(), 14), 2)) == "E" do
    for arp <- [
          [:d4, :f4, :a4, :d5],
          [:d4, :f4, :bb4, :d5],
          [:c4, :f4, :a4, :c5],
          [:c4, :e4, :g4, :c5]
        ] do
      play_pattern_timed arp ++ arp, 0.25, amp: 0.4, release: 0.2
    end
  else
    sleep 8
  end
end

live_loop :pad do
  use_bpm 100
  use_synth "gm_string_ensemble_1"
  play tick([:d3, :bb3, :f3, :c4]), release: 2, amp: 0.8
  sleep 2
end
```

## A MIDI file, heard

Point the cell at a `.mid` file and it becomes a `TuningFork.Score` — tempo map, note
lengths, velocity and pitch bend kept, drums guessed by channel — rendered to audio. A
relative path is taken from the notebook's own directory; `bwv971.mid` sits next to this
one. The written source shows the `TuningFork.Midi.read!/1` and `Gm.score/2` calls it
stands for.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"eyJicG0iOiIiLCJkcnVtX2dhaW4iOiIxLjAiLCJkcnVtcyI6ImF1dG8iLCJnYWluIjoiMC4yIiwibG9vcHMiOiIxIiwicGF0aCI6ImJ3djk3MS5taWQiLCJwbGFpbiI6ZmFsc2UsInJldmVyYiI6IjAuMiIsInZhcmlhYmxlIjoic2NvcmUifQ","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.KinoTuningFork.MidiCell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
score =
  "bwv971.mid"
  |> TuningFork.Midi.read!()
  |> TuningFork.Gm.score(gain: 0.2, drum_gain: 1, drum_channels: :auto)

score
|> TuningFork.Score.render(44100)
|> TuningFork.Fx.reverb(44100, room: 0.2, mix: 0.22)
|> TuningFork.Wav.encode(rate: 44100)
|> Kino.Audio.new(:wav)
```

## The same things from code

Everything a cell does is a call in `TuningFork`. Module names are written out below because
the cells above have already imported `TuningFork.Part` and `TuningFork.SonicPi`, whose
`gain`, `note` and `scale` would clash with `TuningFork.Pattern.Control`'s. A stage in the
notebook:

```elixir
KinoTuningFork.stage()
```

The tune's drums started on it as a pattern, the bass added at the next cycle line, and
stopped:

```elixir
alias TuningFork.Pattern.Control

drums =
  Control.s("<[bd ~] [[bd ~ bd ~] ~] [bd ~] [[bd ~ bd ~] [~ bd ~ ~]]>, ~ sd, <hh*4 [hh hh hh oh]>")
  |> Control.bank("RolandTR909")
  |> Control.gain(0.4)

TuningFork.Stage.start_pattern(drums, cps: 0.8333)
```

```elixir
bass =
  Control.n("<[0 0 4] [5 5 2] [2 2 6] [6 6 3]>")
  |> Control.scale("d2:minor")
  |> Control.struct("x@2 ~ x x@2 x ~")
  |> Control.s("gm_electric_bass_finger")
  |> Control.gain(0.5)

TuningFork.Stage.update_pattern(TuningFork.Pattern.stack([drums, bass]), at: :cycle)
```

```elixir
TuningFork.Stage.stop_pattern()
```

The flute line and the chords as a score, rendered to a WAV the notebook plays without any
stage:

```elixir
alias TuningFork.{Gm, Part, Score, Voice, Wav}

base = Voice.new(shape: :saw, gain: 0.4, cutoff: 0.45)

flute =
  Part.part(bpm: 100, synth: Gm.for_program(73, base))
  |> Part.pattern([:a4, :f4, :d4, :f4], [0.75, 0.25, 0.5, 0.5])
  |> Part.pattern([:g4, :f4, :d4], [0.75, 0.25, 1.0])
  |> Part.pattern([:f4, :a4, :c5, :a4], [0.5, 0.5, 0.75, 0.25])
  |> Part.pattern([:g4, :e4], [1.0, 1.0])

chords =
  [[:d3, :f3, :a3], [:d3, :f3, :bb3], [:c3, :f3, :a3], [:c3, :e3, :g3]]
  |> Enum.reduce(Part.part(bpm: 100, synth: Gm.for_program(4, base)), fn notes, part ->
    part |> Part.chord(notes, 1.5) |> Part.chord(notes, 0.5)
  end)

Score.from_parts([flute, chords], bpm: 100, beats: 8)
|> Score.render(44_100)
|> Wav.encode(rate: 44_100)
|> Kino.Audio.new(:wav)
```
